1646	73	15	12	AI-Augmented Workflows — Analysis & Engineering	{"Day": "Day 12", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Session Title": "AI-Augmented Workflows — Analysis & Engineering  🔴 MILESTONE 2", "Hands-On / Workshop": "Compress a standard 2-hour analysis or build task into 30–45 minutes using Claude and Gemini — a full validation log is required alongside the output.", "Assessment / Assignment": "🔴 MILESTONE 2 (Day 12 End): banking scenario — AI-augmented analysis or build task with a mandatory AI usage log. 90 min. FORMAT: 70% CLOSED-BOOK (AI limitations, validation concepts, data-modelling and warehousing recall) / 30% OPEN-BOOK (the AI-assisted task itself). AI MILESTONE BOT 2 conversational checkpoint. ≥70% to pass; ≥60% minimum.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Payments analytics sprint — AI-assisted insight generation", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "AI as co-analyst and co-engineer — measure the time saved and the accuracy maintained against purely manual work. AI Milestone Bot 2 runs at end of day.", "Core Topics — Live Session": "AI-augmented analytics — accelerating EDA and insight generation · AI-assisted spec and test scaffolding for engineering tasks · AI-assisted data-model and SQL review · validating AI output: where to trust and where to verify · building a repeatable human-in-the-loop workflow", "Discipline Thread (Spec-First / Test-First)": "The AI Usage Log entries from Days 11–12 are reviewed as part of the milestone — the disclosure habit is now assessed.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "AI validation checklist"}
1647	73	16	13	Banking & Payments Domain Deep-Dive	{"Day": "Day 13", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Session Title": "Banking & Payments Domain Deep-Dive", "Hands-On / Workshop": "Analyse a banking product P&L · map the payments data landscape for a regional bank · identify 5 use cases with measurable business value and state the data model each one would require.", "Assessment / Assignment": "3-page opportunity assessment — use cases, data requirements, estimated ROI, risks.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Payments analytics opportunity mapping for a private bank", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "Use Gemini to identify opportunities from a business description and validate them against domain knowledge. Claude pressure-tests the ROI logic.", "Core Topics — Live Session": "Banking business model — revenue streams and cost drivers · payments ecosystem — card rails, digital wallets, UPI/SWIFT · core use cases — churn, credit risk, fraud, CLV · KYC/AML regulatory landscape · AI ethics and regulatory context (GDPR, RBI, DPDP) · how domain shapes the data model — revisiting the Day 9 warehouse with regulatory retention, audit and lineage requirements", "Discipline Thread (Spec-First / Test-First)": "The opportunity assessment must state its success criteria before the analysis begins — the habit applied to a business deliverable.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "Banking regulatory landscape primer"}
1648	73	17	14	Git, GitLab & Stakeholder Management	{"Day": "Day 14", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Session Title": "Git, GitLab & Stakeholder Management  🆕 NEW", "Hands-On / Workshop": "Every learner sets up their programme repository on GitLab — it becomes the single submission channel for the rest of the programme. Branch from main, commit the Day 9–10 model and pipeline work, open a Merge Request, review a peer's MR against a checklist, deliberately create and resolve a three-way merge conflict, protect main and enforce approval before merge. MBA learners run the identical flow for documents and analysis artefacts — repository and review literacy is required of both tracks. Stakeholder simulation: manage a difficult client call on a project running late.", "Assessment / Assignment": "REPOSITORY-BASED ASSIGNMENT: each learner's repository is assessed — branch hygiene, commit-message quality, MR description completeness, review comments given to peers, a conflict resolved cleanly and no secrets in history. From this day ALL assignments are repository-submitted and repository quality is scored. Stakeholder role-play scored separately on communication and resolution.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Banking analytics repository — team branching and merge-request workflow", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "Claude reviews a diff and drafts the MR description → the learner corrects it and owns the final text. Claude used as a code-review assistant on a peer's branch; every AI comment must be validated before it is posted.", "Core Topics — Live Session": "NEW MODULE (client feedback) — the deep session, building on the Day 2 repository-literacy touch. GIT: working tree, staging, commits, history · clone/fetch/pull/push, .gitignore, tags, stash · rebase vs merge, cherry-pick, revert vs reset, conflict resolution · BRANCHING STRATEGIES: trunk-based, Git Flow, GitLab Flow, release and hotfix branches · MERGING: fast-forward, merge commit, squash, rebase-and-merge · GITLAB: projects and groups, Merge Requests, approval rules, protected branches, CODEOWNERS, issues and boards, artefact registry · REPOSITORY MANAGEMENT: monorepo vs polyrepo, README/ADR standards, semantic versioning, changelogs, secrets hygiene · PIPELINE & CHANGE MANAGEMENT: what a pipeline is, how a change moves from branch to production, review gates, release notes and rollback as a change-control discipline · COLLABORATION: code-review etiquette, review checklists, commit conventions. Plus stakeholder mapping (power/interest), managing expectations and escalation, and BI strategy fundamentals.", "Discipline Thread (Spec-First / Test-First)": "The MERGE REQUEST DESCRIPTION is the spec. No MR is approved unless it states what changed, why, what was tested and how to verify it — the discipline thread now has an enforcement mechanism that persists to Day 24.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "Git & GitLab command reference · branching-strategy decision guide · code-review checklist · difficult conversations toolkit"}
1649	73	18	15	Common Capstone Bridge — Part 1: Framing & Build	{"Day": "Day 15", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Session Title": "Common Capstone Bridge — Part 1: Framing & Build\\nMasterclass (Friday — 90 mins)", "Hands-On / Workshop": "Mixed pods (Engineering + MBA) tackle one unseen banking dataset together — Engineering builds the model and pipeline, MBA frames the business narrative, both against a shared spec, all on a shared GitLab repository with protected main and mandatory MR review.", "Assessment / Assignment": "Mid-sprint checkpoint — shared spec quality, business framing and repository hygiene reviewed by the trainer. Not graded into the final score. Scored separately by discipline.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Unseen Straive banking dataset — joint sprint", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "Claude and Gemini assist both lenses — usage logged and disclosed by both sub-teams in the repository.", "Core Topics — Live Session": "Bridge project brief: one Straive banking dataset, two lenses (analytical and technical) · joint hypothesis framing across both disciplines · the pod must produce a data model with declared grain and a warehouse layer plan before any analysis or code begins", "Discipline Thread (Spec-First / Test-First)": "GATING REQUIREMENT: the Engineering sub-team must submit a written mini-spec (including grain statements) plus a failing test BEFORE touching implementation code. The MBA sub-team must submit a hypothesis tree BEFORE starting analysis. No exceptions. CLARIFICATION: Engineering is scored on spec and test artefact quality; MBA is scored independently on hypothesis-tree and business-narrative quality — no MBA learner is evaluated on code or technical build output at any point.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "Common Capstone Bridge brief walkthrough"}
1650	73	19	16	Common Capstone Bridge — Part 2: Present & Transition	{"Day": "Day 16", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Session Title": "Common Capstone Bridge — Part 2: Present & Transition", "Hands-On / Workshop": "Pod presentation to a trainer panel: 10-minute joint delivery plus Q&A · individual reflection on specialization readiness · 1:1 scorecard review with the trainer.", "Assessment / Assignment": "Joint bridge evaluation — feeds cohort readiness and segmentation for the split; not graded into the final score. FULL 360° ROUND: self-assessment + peer ratings + trainer assessment + AI-bot competency read + platform analytics, consolidated onto each Individual Scorecard and shared with Straive L&D.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Full bridge sprint — banking dataset, dual lens", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "The AI usage log across the full Common Track is consolidated for Straive L&D. The AI progress bot generates an individual competency summary per learner.", "Core Topics — Live Session": "Joint presentation — technical build and business recommendation delivered as one narrative · readiness review for the Day 17 track split · Intermediate Proficiency benchmark check-in · mid-programme 360° review", "Discipline Thread (Spec-First / Test-First)": "16-day discipline-thread recap: the trainer panel explicitly reviews each pod's spec-before-work artefacts from Days 1, 3, 5, 9, 10, 14 and 15 as part of the readiness conversation.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "Track-split orientation — what changes from Day 17"}
1651	74	4	17	Spec-First Engineering & Cloud Architecture — AWS / Azure	{"Day": "Day 17", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Core Topics": "EXPANDED per client feedback — cloud services, architecture patterns and deployment approaches now documented explicitly. OpenAPI/Swagger contracts · Pydantic type contracts · C4 architecture spec for a full data platform (ingestion → lakehouse → API) · acceptance-criteria/Gherkin scenarios folded into the spec package. CLOUD SERVICE MAP (AWS ↔ Azure): compute (EC2/Lambda/ECS/EKS ↔ VM/Functions/Container Apps/AKS) · storage (S3 ↔ ADLS Gen2/Blob) · warehouse (Redshift ↔ Synapse/Fabric) · lakehouse (EMR/Glue/Athena ↔ Databricks/Data Factory) · streaming (Kinesis/MSK ↔ Event Hubs) · identity (IAM ↔ Entra ID) · secrets (Secrets Manager ↔ Key Vault) · monitoring (CloudWatch ↔ Azure Monitor). ARCHITECTURE PATTERNS: layered/n-tier, microservices, event-driven, CQRS, hub-and-spoke, landing zone, well-architected pillars. DEPLOYMENT APPROACHES: manual → IaC (Terraform/Bicep/CloudFormation), blue-green, canary, rolling, immutable infrastructure, Dev→Test→Prod promotion. Sizing, vertical vs horizontal scaling and cost modelling.", "Session Title": "Spec-First Engineering & Cloud Architecture — AWS / Azure", "Hands-On Build": "Write an OpenAPI spec + Pydantic models + C4 diagram for a banking data platform spanning ingestion, lakehouse and serving-API layers · produce a cloud service-selection matrix (AWS option vs Azure option with the reason for each choice) · produce a target deployment topology with environments, network boundaries and secret management · size the platform and estimate monthly cost.", "Assessment / Assignment": "Spec package (OpenAPI ≥6 endpoints, Pydantic models, C4 diagram, embedded acceptance criteria, cloud service matrix, deployment topology, cost estimate) — scored on completeness, testability, security definitions AND architecture/scalability reasoning. Repository-submitted via Merge Request.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Banking data platform — spec-first design, ingestion to serving layer, on AWS and Azure", "Methodology (TDD/SDD/AI)": "SDD · AI⚡", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "Claude generates an initial spec and architecture draft → the engineer audits it for missing error codes, ambiguous schemas, security gaps and scaling blind spots. Gemini compares AWS and Azure service options and drafts the architecture decision record.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "OpenAPI & Pydantic reference · AWS ↔ Azure service equivalence map · Well-Architected / CAF summary · IaC and deployment-strategy primer"}
1652	74	5	18	Modern Data Architecture & Data Modeling for Engineers	{"Day": "Day 18", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Core Topics": "NEW MODULE (client feedback). DATA MODELING FUNDAMENTALS for the Engineering track, building on Common Day 9: physical modelling and DDL, indexing and partitioning strategy, constraint design, handling late-arriving and out-of-order facts, modelling for scale. MODERN DATA ARCHITECTURE: DATA MESH — the four principles (domain-oriented ownership, data-as-a-product, self-serve platform, federated computational governance), data product contracts and output ports, when mesh helps and when it is overhead · DATA VAULT 2.0 — hubs, links, satellites, business keys, hash keys, insert-only history, raw vault vs business vault, Vault → star-schema information marts, why Vault suits regulated banking · comparison matrix: Kimball vs Inmon vs Data Vault vs Lakehouse/Medallion vs Mesh, and how to choose · governance, catalogue, lineage and contract enforcement. ARCHITECTURAL USE CASES (banking): real-time fraud detection, auditable regulatory reporting, multi-country source consolidation, decentralised domain analytics. REAL-WORLD DEPLOYMENT SCENARIOS: greenfield cloud build, legacy on-prem migration, hybrid co-existence, incremental strangler-fig migration, cutover and rollback planning.", "Session Title": "Modern Data Architecture & Data Modeling for Engineers  🆕 NEW", "Hands-On Build": "Re-express the Common Day 9 banking model three ways — as a Kimball star, as a Data Vault (hubs/links/satellites for customer, account, transaction, merchant) and as a Data Mesh with two domain data products and published contracts — then write a 2-page architecture decision record recommending ONE for a named banking scenario, with migration path and risks · walk one architectural use case end to end and present the target architecture to the group.", "Assessment / Assignment": "The Architecture Decision Record is both the deliverable and the spec: context → options → decision → consequences. Scored on the quality of the reasoning and the honesty of the trade-offs, not on picking a \\"right\\" answer. Repository-submitted.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Multi-source banking data platform — architecture selection and migration plan", "Methodology (TDD/SDD/AI)": "SDD", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "Claude argues the case FOR each architecture in turn and the engineer must find the weakness in each — adversarial use of AI for design review. Gemini researches reference architectures and summarises vendor documentation.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "Data Mesh primer · Data Vault 2.0 modelling guide · architecture selection matrix · migration patterns"}
1653	74	6	19	TDD + AI-Paired Build & Cloud Data Engineering	{"Day": "Day 19", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Core Topics": "Red→Green→Refactor as the only permitted flow · AI pair-programming protocol — AI suggests, the engineer decides (Claude Code / Gemini Code Assist; GitHub Copilot removed per client direction) · mocking and contract testing (Pact) · testing the dimensional model: grain tests, referential-integrity tests, SCD behaviour tests. CLOUD DATA ENGINEERING: Spark — distributed compute model, DataFrame API, transformations vs actions, lazy evaluation, partitioning, shuffles, skew, broadcast joins, reading the Spark UI · Databricks — clusters, notebooks, Delta Lake (ACID, time travel, MERGE/upsert, OPTIMIZE), Unity Catalog and lineage, job scheduling · Azure Data Factory / AWS Glue — pipelines, activities, triggers, orchestration, retries and SLAs · incremental and CDC loading into the medallion layers · front-end consumption of the platform API (HTML/JS with D3.js and Chart.js) so the pipeline has a visible endpoint. Practice only — full room to fail and retry.", "Session Title": "TDD + AI-Paired Build & Cloud Data Engineering  📘 Practice Day (Ungraded)", "Hands-On Build": "Implement to pass the Day 17 API spec, tests written first, via reviewed Merge Requests · rebuild the Common Track analytics as a Spark job on Databricks orchestrated by Data Factory, landing the Day 9 star schema in Delta Lake with an incremental MERGE · deliberately create a skewed join, diagnose it in the Spark UI and fix it · stand up a lightweight front-end that renders two charts from the API.", "Assessment / Assignment": "Working test-first implementation + Spark job + Delta star-schema tables + Data Factory pipeline + a front-end consuming the API, demonstrated to the trainer and pushed to the repository — feedback given, not scored. Redo encouraged the same day.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Banking transactions lakehouse — batch and incremental ingestion to a curated star schema", "Methodology (TDD/SDD/AI)": "TDD · SDD · AI⚡", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "Claude Code suggests test scaffolds and implementation — the engineer rewrites the assertions to test real behaviour rather than happy paths. Claude also generates a pipeline definition, which is audited for inefficient joins, missing partitioning and cost-blind choices.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "Pytest, mocking & data-quality testing reference · Spark, Databricks, Delta Lake & Data Factory quick reference"}
1654	74	7	20	DevOps, CI/CD & Secure Engineering	{"Day": "Day 20", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Core Topics": "NEW MODULE (client feedback). CI/CD FUNDAMENTALS: what continuous integration actually guarantees, pipeline stages, build/test/scan/package/deploy, fail-fast design, artefacts and caching · GITLAB CI: .gitlab-ci.yml, stages, jobs, runners, rules and needs, environments, protected variables, manual approval gates, pipeline-as-code alongside the Day 14 repository · JENKINS: controller/agent architecture, freestyle vs pipeline jobs, Jenkinsfile and declarative pipelines, shared libraries, credentials management, GitLab webhook integration, why an enterprise runs Jenkins alongside GitLab CI · PIPELINE & CHANGE MANAGEMENT: promoting a change from branch to production, review gates, release notes, rollback as change control · CONTAINERISATION: Dockerfile authoring, layers and caching, multi-stage builds, registries · DEPLOYMENT FUNDAMENTALS: environment promotion, config and secrets per environment, schema migrations, blue-green / canary / rolling, health checks, smoke tests, observability and rollback · IaC basics (Terraform/Bicep). SECURITY FUNDAMENTALS: SECURE CODING — OWASP Top 10 with banking examples, input validation, parameterised queries, authn/authz (RBAC/ABAC), secrets management, cryptography basics, secure logging and PII handling · SAST — how static analysis works, running GitLab SAST / SonarQube / Semgrep in the pipeline, triaging true vs false positives, quality gates that block a merge · SCA, dependency and container scanning (Trivy), SBOM · DAST awareness (OWASP ZAP) · VULNERABILITY ASSESSMENT — asset inventory, scanning, CVSS scoring, prioritisation, remediation SLAs, and how a VA differs from a pen test · PENETRATION TESTING BASICS — the phases (recon → scanning → exploitation → reporting), rules of engagement and legal boundaries, reading a pen-test report and converting findings into a remediation backlog · STRIDE threat modelling · DevSecOps: shifting security left.", "Session Title": "DevOps, CI/CD & Secure Engineering  🆕 NEW  📘 Practice Day (Ungraded)\\nMasterclass (Friday — 90 mins)", "Hands-On Build": "DEPLOYMENT EXERCISES: (1) write a .gitlab-ci.yml that lints, tests, builds a Docker image and pushes it to the registry — then make a test fail and prove the deploy is blocked; (2) write a Jenkinsfile performing the same build/test/package flow, triggered from a GitLab webhook; (3) containerise the Day 17–19 API with a multi-stage Dockerfile and deploy it to a cloud environment; (4) run a schema migration inside the pipeline; (5) perform a blue-green deployment and then execute a deliberate rollback under time pressure; (6) add a manual approval gate before production. SECURITY EXERCISES: run SAST and dependency/container scanning against your own code and fix the real findings · wire SAST + secret detection + container scan into the pipeline and configure it to BLOCK the merge on a high-severity finding · run OWASP ZAP against the deployed API and triage the output · complete a STRIDE threat model · write a CVSS-prioritised vulnerability assessment report · guided walkthrough of a pen-test report against a deliberately vulnerable banking app.", "Assessment / Assignment": "Working GitLab CI pipeline AND a working Jenkinsfile in the learner's repository, image published, application deployed, one rollback demonstrated with committed evidence, SAST/secret-detection/dependency scanning wired in as a blocking gate with all high-severity findings remediated, plus a STRIDE threat model and a VA report. Practice day — reviewed and feedback given, not scored, but this is the direct rehearsal for Milestone 3.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Banking data API and pipeline — automated build, test, containerise, secure, deploy and roll back", "Methodology (TDD/SDD/AI)": "SDD · AI⚡", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "Claude generates first-draft pipeline and Jenkinsfile definitions → the engineer audits for skippable test stages, hard-coded secrets, missing failure conditions and unsafe deploy steps. Claude reviews code for security flaws and the engineer verifies each finding against the scanner output, rejecting false positives.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "GitLab CI reference · Jenkins & Jenkinsfile primer · Dockerfile best practices · deployment and rollback playbook · OWASP Top 10 · SAST/DAST/SCA tooling guide · CVSS and VA reporting template · STRIDE worksheet"}
1655	74	8	21	Full-Stack Data Platform Milestone — Model, Pipeline, CI/CD & Security	{"Day": "Day 21", "Hours": "8", "Difficulty": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐", "Core Topics": "No new content — this is the graded assessment day, one full day after the last new material, so learners arrive with a night of SPL reinforcement rather than a same-day cram-then-test.", "Session Title": "Full-Stack Data Platform Milestone — Model, Pipeline, CI/CD & Security  🔴 MILESTONE 3 (Assessment Only)", "Hands-On Build": "Live demo on an unseen banking scenario: a dimensional model with declared grain (Day 9) landing in a medallion lakehouse via a Spark/Data Factory flow (Day 19), exposed through a test-gated API, built and deployed by a GitLab CI + Jenkins pipeline with a blocking SAST/secret-scan gate (Day 20), consumed by a front-end, with a demonstrated rollback — full stack, end to end, from the learner's own repository.", "Assessment / Assignment": "🔴 MILESTONE 3 (Day 21 End): live demo plus defence. 2.5 hrs. FORMAT: 70% CLOSED-BOOK (architecture, data-modelling, CI/CD and security concept viva with no tools) / 30% OPEN-BOOK (the build and deploy itself, with full tool and AI access — the pipeline either goes green or it does not). A failing test must block deployment · the pipeline must run · the SAST gate must block a planted vulnerability · the container must build · a rollback must be demonstrated. AI MILESTONE BOT 3 runs a technical checkpoint on the repository (commit history, MR quality, AI usage log). ≥70% to pass; ≥60% minimum.", "Banking Domain Use Case": "Banking data platform — model to dashboard, test-, pipeline- and security-enforced", "Methodology (TDD/SDD/AI)": "TDD · SDD", "AI Integration Touchpoint": "The AI usage log from Days 17–20 is reviewed as part of scoring — the disclosure habit is assessed across data-modelling, data-engineering, pipeline and security artefacts.", "SPL — Self-Paced Topic (not counted in hours)": "Milestone 3 readiness checklist"}
